# Striatum: Claim Loop

This is the core of driving a Striatum run. You will repeat this
loop until the run hits a terminal state.

## When to invoke

- The operator asked to claim work / drive a run that's already
  `ready` or `running`.
- A work packet was returned by `claim-next` and you need to act
  on it.
- A required artifact needs to be published.

## Authoritative reference

{verbs_claim_loop}

Run `striatum <verb> --help` for full flag sets.

## Common patterns

```bash
# 1. Register a session for a (role, lane) pair.
striatum --repo $REPO register-session --run-id $RUN \
    --role $ROLE --lane $LANE --json

# 2. Claim the next packet.
striatum --repo $REPO claim-next --session-id $SESSION --json

# 3. Ack the packet and start work.
striatum --repo $REPO ack --session-id $SESSION \
    --message-id $MSG --lease-id $LEASE --json

# 4. (RFC 0008) If `worktree_required: true`, run the
#    `commands.worktree_create` invocation from the packet
#    BEFORE you publish artifacts.

# 5. Publish each required artifact (build/synthesis/handoff).
striatum --repo $REPO publish-artifact --session-id $SESSION \
    --job-id $JOB --lease-id $LEASE \
    --kind $KIND --logical-name $NAME --path $PATH --json

# 6a. Build job: complete it.
striatum --repo $REPO complete --session-id $SESSION \
    --job-id $JOB --lease-id $LEASE --json

# 6b. Review job: publish a finding + verdict in one call.
striatum --repo $REPO submit-review --session-id $SESSION \
    --job-id $JOB --lease-id $LEASE \
    --kind finding --logical-name $NAME --path $PATH \
    --verdict {{accept,accept_with_findings,needs_revision,reject}} --json

# 7. (RFC 0008) Release the worktree if you created one.
striatum --repo $REPO worktree release --worktree-id $WORKTREE
```

